#!/bin/sh
# Require the BWS token as a precondition for every chezmoi apply, on every
# platform (BOX-174).
#
# Why this exists: several templates resolve their value by shelling out to
# `bws secret get` (gh hosts.yml, the Linear credentials, the git signingkey,
# the .ssh public keys). Those templates are guarded, so a token-less apply
# does not error -- it renders EMPTY values. A hand-run `chezmoi apply` on a
# host whose token the invocation did not supply therefore silently blanks
# ~/.config/gh/hosts.yml's oauth_token and the Linear API keys, destroying
# credentials a correct apply had installed.
#
# Checking the token once, here, makes that impossible: a `run_before` script
# that exits non-zero aborts the whole apply before any template is rendered,
# so no template can ever write a degraded value. Doing it here also means the
# individual templates need no token guard of their own.
#
# The token reaches the environment differently per host class, but the rule is
# identical on both: if it is not available, fail.
#   - headless: the Home Manager activation (home-manager-podman.service)
#     delivers it via LoadCredential and reads it from $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY.
#   - desktop/macOS: ~/.config/shell/secrets.sh reads it from the login
#     keychain. Note that a token that is *absent* is a failure here too: the
#     normal source is the keychain, so absence means either the keychain has
#     no entry or the shell never sourced secrets.sh.
{{ if .headless -}}
if [ -z "${BWS_ACCESS_TOKEN:-}" ]; then
    cat >&2 <<'MSG'
FATAL: BWS_ACCESS_TOKEN is not set — refusing to apply.

This is a headless host; the token is delivered by the Home Manager
activation. Apply through it:

    just switch
MSG
    exit 1
fi
{{ else -}}
if [ -z "${BWS_ACCESS_TOKEN:-}" ]; then
    cat >&2 <<'MSG'
FATAL: BWS_ACCESS_TOKEN is not set — refusing to apply.

The token normally comes from the login keychain via
~/.config/shell/secrets.sh. Its absence means either the keychain has no
`bws_access_token` entry, or this shell never sourced secrets.sh.

Repopulate the keychain entry, then apply from an interactive shell:

    ~/.local/bin/setup-bws-keyring.sh
MSG
    exit 1
fi
{{ end -}}
